    "modelAnswer": "Oxygen enters through the nose, where hairs, mucus and cilia filter the air and the nasal cavity warms and moistens it. It passes through the pharynx and the larynx into the trachea, which is held open by cartilage rings, then down a bronchus and along narrower bronchioles to an alveolus. There it diffuses across the one-cell-thick wall into the blood and is carried by haemoglobin to the body cells, where it is used in the mitochondria to release energy from glucose (internal respiration), while carbon dioxide travels back the other way. All of this is driven by the diaphragm: when it contracts and flattens and the ribs lift, the chest volume rises and the pressure drops, so air flows in."
